{"source":"user-event","type":"click","text":"A\n    \n      New mode on existing Root\n      A view=\"weeks\" prop on Root switches grid rendering\n      \n        \n          Pros\n          \n            Single entry point — consumers learn one API\n            Shared state is automatic (selection, disabled dates, min/max all just work)\n            Existing components like DayButton, DayCell stay the same\n            Switching between views at runtime is trivial (toggle a prop)\n          \n        \n        \n          Cons\n          \n            Root's props/state grow — weeks-only props (weekCount, firstWeek, overflow behavior) clutter the API for month-grid users\n            Grid component needs branching logic for two rendering modes\n            New components (month separators, week nav buttons) only make sense in weeks mode — consumers could misuse them in month mode\n            Harder to tree-shake — consumers who only want month-grid still bundle weeks logic\n            Risk of the Root becoming a \"god component\" as features accumulate","choice":"a","id":null,"timestamp":1774370401650}
{"source":"user-event","type":"click","text":"B\n    \n      Separate compound component tree\n      New top-level component (e.g. WeeksView.Root) with its own components, sharing context with DatePicker\n      \n        \n          Pros\n          \n            Clean separation — each view's API only exposes what's relevant\n            No branching in Grid — each view has its own grid component purpose-built for its layout\n            New components (separators, week nav) live naturally under the new namespace\n            Tree-shakeable — don't use it, don't bundle it\n            Easier path to infinite scroll later — the weeks view can evolve independently\n            Shared selection/state still possible via shared context or composition\n          \n        \n        \n          Cons\n          \n            More API surface to learn — two component trees instead of one\n            Shared logic (day rendering, keyboard nav, selection) needs to be extracted into shared hooks/utils to avoid duplication\n            Runtime view switching requires swapping component trees rather than toggling a prop\n            Risk of the two views drifting apart in behavior if shared logic isn't well-factored","choice":"b","id":null,"timestamp":1774370445658}
{"type":"screen-added","file":"/Users/klink/localdev/base-ui-cal/.superpowers/brainstorm/10375-1774370011/root-strategy.html"}
{"source":"user-event","type":"click","text":"C\n    \n      Shared CalendarProvider extracted from Root\n      <CalendarProvider value={...} min={...}><WeeksView weekCount={6}>...</WeeksView></CalendarProvider>\n      \n        \n          Pros\n          \n            Single source of truth for shared state — selection, min/max, locale, disabled dates defined once\n            Each view only adds its own props — clean separation of concerns\n            Both views can coexist and stay in sync automatically (shared context)\n            Forces clean factoring — shared vs view-specific logic is explicit\n            Natural extension point for future views (year view, agenda view, etc.)\n          \n        \n        \n          Cons\n          \n            Breaking change — existing <Root> API must change or become a convenience wrapper\n            More components to understand — three layers instead of one (Provider → View Root → Components)\n            Over-engineered if only two views will ever exist\n            Requires deciding upfront exactly which state is \"shared\" vs \"view-specific\" — get it wrong and you're shuffling props later","choice":"c","id":null,"timestamp":1774370851601}
